<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ratae Corieltauvorum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ratae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ratae Corieltauvorum was the Roman town on the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the capital of the civitas of the Corieltauvi. It grew from a late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlement and a Roman fort into a planned town with a forum and basilica, public baths and town walls. The Jewry Wall, part of the 2nd-century baths complex, is one of the tallest surviving pieces of Roman masonry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ratae-corieltauvorum.jpg" alt="Ratae Corieltauvorum" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Stukeley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stukeley_Leicester_Map_1722.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
